-- certifications_accounting.sql
-- Why: the founder's ruling of 6 Sep 2026 is that every position sits the real certification exams of its
-- profession, and an agent that cannot pass them does not know enough. This file authors the four accounting
-- question banks (CPA, CMA, ACCA, APS) at real exam difficulty: scenario and application questions, four
-- options, one key, a two-sentence rationale, the syllabus area named as the issuing body names it, ~25% 'hard',
-- keys spread evenly across A/B/C/D. Where a syllabus covers tax, a few questions test the UAE variant the
-- company actually operates under (VAT 5% with the AED 375,000 registration threshold, Corporate Tax 9% above
-- AED 375,000 with Small Business Relief, IFRS 15 on the marketplace's 3% fee).
-- Each bank is guarded by `where not exists (... certification = '<name>')` so it is authored once even if two
-- authors share a certification; the first author's bank stands. role_certification rows are a sibling
-- engineer's file and are deliberately NOT written here.
--
-- Apply: node scripts/apply_sql.mjs scripts/sql/certifications_accounting.sql --apply --ddl

-- ============================================================================
-- CPA (Certified Public Accountant) — AICPA/NASBA
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Financial Reporting','exam','A marketplace operator introduces buyers to suppliers and charges the supplier 3% of the contract value once the supplier and buyer transact. The operator never controls the goods, sets no price and carries no inventory risk. How should it present revenue?','Net, recognising only the 3% fee as revenue because it acts as an agent','Gross contract value as revenue with the supplier payment as cost of sales','Gross contract value, because the buyer contracts through the operator platform','No revenue until the supplier collects cash from the buyer','A','Under IFRS 15 / ASC 606 the operator is an agent: it does not obtain control of the specified good before transfer, does not set price and bears no inventory risk. An agent recognises revenue at the net amount of consideration it retains, here the 3% fee.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Financial Reporting','exam','An entity signs a two-year contract with a customer that includes a licence delivered on day one and two years of unspecified updates. The licence sells separately for 800 and the update service for 400; the bundle price is 1,000. How much is allocated to the licence?','1,000','667','800','500','B','The transaction price is allocated to performance obligations in proportion to standalone selling prices: 800 / 1,200 x 1,000 = 667. The discount is spread across both obligations because nothing indicates it relates to only one.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Select Balance Sheet Accounts','exam','A company has a receivable ageing where 30 days and under is 500,000 with 1% expected loss, 31-90 days is 200,000 with 6%, and over 90 days is 100,000 with 25%. The allowance currently carries a credit balance of 12,000. What is the bad debt expense for the period?','54,000','42,000','30,000','12,000','C','The required allowance is 5,000 + 12,000 + 25,000 = 42,000. Because the account already carries a 12,000 credit, only the top-up of 30,000 is charged to expense.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Select Balance Sheet Accounts','exam','An entity capitalises development costs of 900,000 for an internally generated platform with a five-year life, placed in service on 1 July. Under the straight-line method with no residual value, what is amortisation for that first calendar year?','180,000','45,000','900,000','90,000','D','Annual amortisation is 900,000 / 5 = 180,000, and only half the year has elapsed since the asset was available for use. Amortisation begins when the asset is ready for its intended use, giving 90,000.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Select Transactions','hard','A lessee signs a five-year lease with annual payments of 100,000 in arrears and an incremental borrowing rate of 8%. The present value factor for an ordinary annuity of five years at 8% is 3.9927. The lessee also pays 20,000 of initial direct costs. What is the initial right-of-use asset?','419,270','399,270','500,000','520,000','A','The lease liability is 100,000 x 3.9927 = 399,270. The right-of-use asset equals the liability plus initial direct costs, 399,270 + 20,000 = 419,270.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Select Transactions','exam','A company receives a non-refundable upfront platform activation fee from a supplier that does not transfer a distinct good or service. The expected relationship lasts three years. How is the fee treated?','Recognised immediately as revenue on receipt','Deferred and recognised over the period the related services are provided','Recognised as a liability until the contract is cancelled','Credited directly to equity as a contract acquisition','B','A non-refundable upfront fee that does not transfer a distinct good or service is an advance payment for future goods or services. It is deferred and recognised as those future services are delivered, over the expected customer relationship.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Financial Reporting','exam','An entity discovers that depreciation in the prior year was understated by a material amount because a useful life was entered incorrectly into the fixed asset register. How is this reported?','Prospectively as a change in accounting estimate','As a change in accounting policy applied retrospectively with a policy note','As a prior period error corrected by restating comparative figures','In other comprehensive income in the current period','C','An input mistake is an error, not a revision of an estimate made on the information then available. Material prior period errors are corrected retrospectively by restating the comparative amounts.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Financial Reporting','hard','A parent holds 80% of a subsidiary whose net assets at acquisition had a fair value of 1,000,000. Consideration transferred was 900,000 and the non-controlling interest is measured at its proportionate share of net assets. What goodwill arises?','Nothing; a gain on bargain purchase of 100,000','200,000 of goodwill','900,000 of goodwill','100,000 of goodwill','D','The non-controlling interest at proportionate share is 20% x 1,000,000 = 200,000. Goodwill is 900,000 + 200,000 - 1,000,000 = 100,000.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Assessing Risk and Developing a Planned Response','exam','During planning the auditor learns that a marketplace client changed its revenue recognition from gross to net mid-year following a contract review. What is the most appropriate response?','Treat revenue recognition as a significant risk and design specific procedures over the agent-principal conclusion','Reduce substantive testing because management corrected the treatment itself','Issue a qualified opinion for the inconsistency between periods','Rely on the internal audit team memo without further work','A','A change in a judgemental revenue conclusion mid-year increases the risk of material misstatement and involves significant management judgement. The auditor identifies it as a significant risk and designs a substantive response addressing the agent-principal analysis.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Assessing Risk and Developing a Planned Response','exam','An auditor sets overall materiality at 500,000 and performance materiality at 350,000. Which statement best describes why performance materiality is lower?','It gives the client a stricter internal reporting threshold','It reduces to an appropriately low level the probability that uncorrected and undetected misstatements together exceed overall materiality','It is required to be exactly 70% of overall materiality','It is the level below which misstatements are clearly trivial','B','Performance materiality is set below overall materiality to leave room for the aggregation of individually immaterial misstatements. It reduces the risk that the total of uncorrected and undetected misstatements exceeds overall materiality.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Performing Further Procedures and Obtaining Evidence','exam','An auditor wants evidence that recorded platform fee revenue actually occurred. Which procedure gives the most reliable evidence of occurrence?','Trace supplier contracts forward to the revenue ledger','Recompute the 3% rate on the total contract value reported by management','Select recorded revenue entries and vouch them to the underlying settled transaction and cash receipt','Enquire of the revenue operations manager about the fee policy','C','Occurrence is tested by starting with the recorded amount and vouching back to supporting evidence. Tracing forward from contracts tests completeness, not occurrence, and enquiry alone is weak evidence.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Performing Further Procedures and Obtaining Evidence','hard','Positive confirmations were sent for 60 receivable balances; 12 did not reply and two replies disagreed with the ledger. What is the appropriate next step for the non-replies?','Treat the non-replies as accepted because no exception was reported','Report a scope limitation in the auditor report','Extend the sample by another 60 confirmations','Perform alternative procedures such as examining subsequent cash receipts and shipping documents','D','Non-responses to positive confirmations provide no evidence and cannot be assumed correct. The auditor performs alternative procedures, typically inspecting subsequent receipts and the underlying delivery documents.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Forming Conclusions and Reporting','exam','The auditor concludes that inventory is materially misstated but the effect is not pervasive to the financial statements as a whole. What opinion is issued?','Qualified opinion','Unmodified with an emphasis of matter paragraph','Adverse opinion','Disclaimer of opinion','A','A material but not pervasive misstatement leads to a qualified "except for" opinion. An adverse opinion is reserved for misstatements that are both material and pervasive.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Forming Conclusions and Reporting','exam','Two weeks after the balance sheet date and before the report is signed, a major customer of the client enters insolvency; the receivable existed at year end. How should this be handled?','Disclose only, because insolvency happened after year end','Adjust the financial statements, because it provides evidence of a condition existing at the reporting date','Ignore it as a subsequent event outside the audit period','Recognise it as a contingent asset','B','The customer credit condition existed at the reporting date and the insolvency confirms it. This is an adjusting event after the reporting period and the receivable allowance is adjusted.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Ethics, Professional Responsibilities and General Principles','exam','An audit partner is offered a contingent fee equal to 2% of the funding a client raises if the audited statements support the raise. Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct this is','permitted if disclosed in the engagement letter','permitted if approved by those charged with governance','prohibited, because contingent fees are not allowed for attest clients','permitted if the fee is capped','C','Contingent fees are prohibited for clients for whom the member performs an attest service. The arrangement creates a self-interest threat that no disclosure or cap can reduce to an acceptable level.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Ethics, Professional Responsibilities and General Principles','hard','A firm provides bookkeeping services and then audits the same private client. Which safeguard, if any, makes this acceptable under independence rules for a public interest entity?','Using different staff for each service','Obtaining written client consent','Charging separate fees for each engagement','No safeguard; management responsibilities and self-review make it prohibited for such an entity','D','Preparing the accounting records the firm will later audit is a self-review threat and involves assuming management responsibility. For a public interest entity no safeguard reduces this to an acceptable level, so the service is prohibited.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','REG — Federal Taxation of Entities','exam','A company incurs a legal expense that is deductible for tax when paid but expensed for accounting when accrued. At year end it is accrued and unpaid. What deferred tax item arises?','A deferred tax asset, because the deduction reverses in a future period','A deferred tax liability, because taxable income is lower now','A permanent difference with no deferred tax','A reduction of goodwill','A','The expense is booked now but deducted later, so accounting profit is lower than taxable profit today and higher later. That deductible temporary difference gives rise to a deferred tax asset.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','REG — Federal Taxation of Entities','exam','A UAE-resident company on the marketplace reports taxable income of AED 500,000 for the period and has not elected Small Business Relief. What is the Corporate Tax charge?','AED 45,000','AED 11,250','AED 0','AED 25,000','B','UAE Corporate Tax is 0% on the first AED 375,000 of taxable income and 9% on the excess. The charge is (500,000 - 375,000) x 9% = AED 11,250.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','REG — Federal Taxation of Entities','hard','The same UAE-resident company has revenue of AED 2.4 million and elects Small Business Relief for the period. What is the effect on its Corporate Tax position?','It pays 9% on all income above AED 375,000','It is exempt from filing a tax return','It is treated as having no taxable income for the period, subject to meeting the revenue threshold and filing requirements','It becomes liable for VAT at 5% instead','C','Small Business Relief lets a resident person with revenue at or below the published threshold elect to be treated as having no taxable income. The return must still be filed and the election made each period.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','REG — Business Law','exam','A supplier signs a contract that its operations manager had no actual authority to sign, but the manager had signed similar contracts for two years with the company honouring them. The counterparty seeks enforcement. What is the strongest basis?','The contract is void for want of authority','The contract is voidable at the option of the supplier only','The manager is personally liable and the company is not bound','Apparent authority arising from the course of dealing the principal permitted','D','A principal that holds a person out as authorised, including through a consistent course of dealing, is bound by that apparent authority. The counterparty who relied reasonably can enforce the contract against the principal.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','BAR — Business Analysis','exam','A platform reports gross transaction value of 20,000,000, take rate 3%, contribution margin 60% of net revenue and fixed costs of 250,000 per month. What is the annual operating profit?','-2,640,000','600,000','360,000','60,000','A','Net revenue is 600,000 a year and contribution is 360,000. Fixed costs of 3,000,000 a year give an operating loss of 2,640,000.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','BAR — Business Analysis','hard','Using the same platform, how much gross transaction value per year is needed to break even, holding the 3% take rate and 60% contribution margin constant?','100,000,000','166,666,667','50,000,000','20,000,000','B','Contribution per unit of GTV is 3% x 60% = 1.8%. Break-even GTV is 3,000,000 / 0.018 = 166,666,667.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','BAR — Business Analysis','exam','An analyst is asked why a rising headline revenue number can coexist with falling cash generation on a marketplace. Which explanation is most consistent with both facts?','The company switched from gross to net presentation','Fixed costs fell during the period','Revenue is recognised on transaction while fee receivables from suppliers lengthen in days outstanding','Deferred revenue was released to income','C','Revenue recognition does not depend on collection, so lengthening receivable days increases revenue while cash lags. The other explanations would not produce rising revenue with falling cash together.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','ISC — Information Systems and Controls','exam','A finance system lets the same user create a supplier record and approve payments to it. What control weakness is this and what is the direct remedy?','Lack of physical security; install access badges','Weak encryption; enable TLS on the database','Poor data retention; extend the archive period','Inadequate segregation of duties; split the create and approve roles across different users','D','One user who can both create a payee and approve payment to it can misappropriate funds without a second party. The remedy is segregation of incompatible duties enforced by system roles.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','ISC — Information Systems and Controls','exam','An auditor tests a SOC 2 report on a subservice organisation that hosts the client accounting data. The report uses the carve-out method. What does the auditor still need?','Evidence about the controls at the carved-out subservice organisation, obtained separately','Nothing further; the carve-out covers the subservice provider','A bridge letter from the client only','The auditor must re-perform the service auditor tests','A','Carve-out means the subservice organisation controls are excluded from the scope of the report. The user auditor must obtain evidence about those controls from another source.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','ISC — Information Systems and Controls','hard','A general IT control over program change management is found to be ineffective for the whole year. What is the primary consequence for the audit approach?','Only manual controls are affected','Automated application controls that depend on that environment cannot be relied on without further work','Materiality must be lowered by half','The auditor must issue a disclaimer','B','Automated controls rely on the integrity of the programs and the change process that protects them. If change management fails, reliance on those automated controls is not supported without additional testing such as baseline comparison.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','TCP — Tax Compliance and Planning','exam','A UAE business makes taxable supplies of AED 410,000 over the past twelve months. What is its VAT obligation?','No registration is needed until AED 1,000,000','Voluntary registration only, because the threshold is AED 500,000','Mandatory VAT registration, because taxable supplies exceeded AED 375,000','Registration only if it imports goods','C','The mandatory UAE VAT registration threshold is AED 375,000 of taxable supplies and imports over the preceding twelve months. Once exceeded the business must register and charge VAT at the standard 5% rate.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','TCP — Tax Compliance and Planning','exam','A VAT-registered UAE company charges a supplier a platform fee of AED 30,000 for services performed in the UAE to a UAE-resident recipient. What VAT applies?','Zero-rated as an export of services','Out of scope, because it is a commission','Exempt as a financial service','Standard-rated at 5%, so AED 1,500 of output VAT','D','Services supplied in the UAE to a UAE recipient are standard-rated unless a specific zero-rating or exemption applies. Platform commission is not a listed exempt financial service, so 5% output VAT of AED 1,500 arises.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','TCP — Tax Compliance and Planning','hard','A company plans to accelerate a deductible expense into the current period. Its taxable income before the expense is AED 380,000 and the expense is AED 20,000. What is the marginal tax saving under UAE Corporate Tax?','AED 450','AED 1,800','AED 0','AED 1,800 plus VAT recovery','A','Only AED 5,000 of income sits above the AED 375,000 zero-rate band, so only that slice is taxed at 9%. The deduction saves 9% on AED 5,000, which is AED 450.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Financial Reporting','exam','A company classifies a five-year loan as non-current at year end. Two weeks after the reporting date but before authorisation of the statements, it breaches a covenant giving the lender the right to demand repayment. How is the loan classified at the reporting date?','Current, because the breach occurred before authorisation','Non-current, because the right to defer settlement existed at the reporting date; the breach is disclosed as a non-adjusting event','Split equally between current and non-current','Derecognised and re-recognised at fair value','B','Classification depends on rights existing at the end of the reporting period. A covenant breach after that date does not change the classification but is disclosed as a non-adjusting event if material.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Select Balance Sheet Accounts','exam','Inventory cost is 100,000, estimated selling price is 115,000, and costs to complete and sell are 20,000. At what amount is inventory carried?','100,000','115,000','95,000','80,000','C','Inventory is measured at the lower of cost and net realisable value. Net realisable value is 115,000 - 20,000 = 95,000, which is below cost, so a write-down of 5,000 is recognised.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Select Transactions','exam','A company issues 1,000 share options to employees with a grant-date fair value of 12 each, vesting over three years with expected forfeiture of 10%. What expense is recognised in year one?','12,000','10,800','4,000','3,600','D','Total expected cost is 1,000 x 12 x 90% = 10,800, spread over the three-year vesting period. Year one expense is 10,800 / 3 = 3,600.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Assessing Risk and Developing a Planned Response','hard','An auditor performs a preliminary analytical procedure and finds that the take rate implied by revenue over gross transaction value fell from 3.0% to 2.4% with no policy change. What is the best interpretation?','A possible indicator of unrecorded revenue or unrecorded fee waivers requiring investigation','A favourable trend requiring no work','Evidence that materiality should be increased','Confirmation that revenue is fairly stated','A','A ratio that moves without an explanation from a policy or pricing change is a risk signal, not a conclusion. The auditor investigates whether revenue is incomplete or whether discounts and waivers were granted outside policy.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Performing Further Procedures and Obtaining Evidence','exam','Which sampling approach is most appropriate to test whether recorded revenue is overstated in a population with many small and a few very large invoices?','Simple random sampling of invoice numbers','Monetary unit sampling, which weights selection by value','Haphazard selection of one invoice per month','Selection of only the smallest invoices','B','Monetary unit sampling gives every currency unit an equal chance of selection, so higher-value items are more likely to be picked. That directs effort where an overstatement would be material.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Forming Conclusions and Reporting','exam','Management refuses to allow the auditor to confirm receivables and offers no alternative records. The receivables are material and pervasive to the balance sheet. What opinion is appropriate?','Qualified opinion','Adverse opinion','Disclaimer of opinion','Unmodified opinion with a key audit matter','C','An inability to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence that is both material and pervasive results in a disclaimer of opinion. An adverse opinion applies to misstatement, not to a scope limitation.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','BAR — Business Analysis','exam','A subsidiary has functional currency EUR and the group presents in AED. Which rate is used to translate the subsidiary income statement for consolidation?','Closing rate at the reporting date','The rate on the last day of the first month','Historical rate at acquisition','Average rate for the period as an approximation of transaction-date rates','D','Income and expenses are translated at the rates at the dates of the transactions, and an average rate is commonly used as a practical approximation. Assets and liabilities use the closing rate, with the difference taken to other comprehensive income.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','ISC — Information Systems and Controls','exam','A company stores supplier bank details in a database that all customer support agents can read. Which control best addresses the confidentiality risk without stopping support work?','Restrict field-level access so support roles see masked account numbers','Encrypt the database backups only','Require a stronger password policy for all users','Log all queries and review them annually','A','The risk is over-broad read access to sensitive data, so the direct control is least-privilege at the field level with masking. Backup encryption, passwords and annual log review do not remove day-to-day exposure.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','TCP — Tax Compliance and Planning','exam','A UAE company receives a supplier invoice with 5% VAT for a business expense used to make taxable supplies. What is the correct treatment?','Expense the VAT as a cost of the purchase','Recover it as input VAT on the return for the period, subject to holding a valid tax invoice','Recover it only when the customer pays','Treat it as a Corporate Tax credit','B','Input VAT on costs used for taxable supplies is recoverable in the period the invoice is received and the intention to pay exists, provided a valid tax invoice is held. It is not a Corporate Tax credit.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','FAR — Financial Reporting','hard','A parent loses control of a subsidiary but retains a 25% equity interest with significant influence. How is the retained interest measured on the date control is lost?','At its share of the former carrying amount of net assets','At original cost','At fair value, with the resulting gain or loss recognised in profit or loss','At the higher of cost and fair value','C','On loss of control the parent derecognises the subsidiary assets and liabilities and remeasures any retained interest at fair value on that date. The difference is recognised in profit or loss and the retained interest becomes the cost of the associate.'),
('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)','AUD — Ethics, Professional Responsibilities and General Principles','exam','An engagement quality reviewer disagrees with the engagement partner conclusion on a significant judgement. What must happen before the report is released?','The partner may sign because the reviewer role is advisory','The client casts the deciding vote','The reviewer resigns from the engagement','The matter is resolved through the firm policy for resolving differences of opinion before the report is dated','D','Firm quality management policies require that differences of opinion be resolved before the report is released. The report cannot be dated while the engagement quality review is unresolved.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CPA (Certified Public Accountant)');

-- ============================================================================
-- CMA (Certified Management Accountant) — IMA
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Cost Management','exam','A platform allocates 600,000 of shared engineering cost using headcount, but 70% of engineering effort goes to a product line with only 30% of headcount. What costing change most improves decision usefulness?','Adopt activity-based costing with an effort or story-point driver','Allocate on revenue instead of headcount','Stop allocating the cost and treat it as a period cost','Allocate equally across all product lines','A','The current driver does not correlate with the resource consumed, so allocations mislead product profitability. Activity-based costing with an effort driver ties cost to the activity that causes it.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Cost Management','exam','Standard materials for a unit are 4 kg at 5 each. Actual output was 2,000 units using 8,400 kg at 4.80 each. What is the materials quantity variance?','1,680 favourable','2,000 unfavourable','2,000 favourable','1,920 unfavourable','B','Standard quantity for actual output is 8,000 kg while actual usage was 8,400 kg, so 400 kg extra is priced at the standard 5 to give 2,000. More was used than standard, so the variance is unfavourable.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Cost Management','hard','Using the same data, what is the materials price variance and who is normally accountable?','800 favourable, production','1,680 unfavourable, production','1,680 favourable, purchasing','2,000 favourable, purchasing','C','The price variance is 8,400 kg x (5.00 - 4.80) = 1,680 favourable. Price variances are normally the responsibility of purchasing, which selects the supplier and negotiates terms.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Performance Management','exam','A division has operating income of 400,000, invested capital of 2,000,000 and a required rate of return of 12%. What is its residual income?','400,000','20 percent','240,000','160,000','D','Residual income is operating income less a capital charge: 400,000 - (12% x 2,000,000) = 160,000. A 20% return on investment is a ratio, not residual income.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Performance Management','hard','The same manager is evaluated on return on investment and rejects a project earning 16% that would use 500,000 of capital. What does this illustrate and what fixes it?','Suboptimisation under ROI; evaluating on residual income or economic value added aligns the decision with the 12% hurdle','Correct behaviour, since 16% is below the hurdle','A capital rationing constraint that nothing can fix','A transfer pricing dispute best solved with market price','A','ROI evaluation penalises a manager whose divisional average is 20% for accepting a 16% project even though 16% beats the 12% cost of capital. Residual income or EVA rewards any project earning above the required return.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Performance Management','exam','Budgeted fixed overhead is 300,000 for 30,000 planned machine hours. Actual output allowed 28,000 standard hours and actual fixed overhead was 305,000. What is the production volume variance?','5,000 unfavourable','20,000 unfavourable','20,000 favourable','25,000 unfavourable','B','The standard fixed rate is 10 per hour, so overhead applied is 280,000 against 300,000 budgeted. The 20,000 shortfall caused by operating below planned volume is an unfavourable volume variance.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Planning, Budgeting and Forecasting','exam','A company builds a cash budget. Sales are 100,000 in March and 120,000 in April; 60% is collected in the month of sale and 40% in the following month. What are April cash collections?','120,000','108,000','112,000','100,000','C','April collections are 60% of April sales plus 40% of March sales: 72,000 + 40,000 = 112,000. Sales recognition and cash collection differ by the credit terms granted.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Planning, Budgeting and Forecasting','exam','Management wants a budget that adjusts allowed costs to the volume actually achieved so that variances isolate efficiency rather than volume. Which budget does this?','Static master budget','Rolling forecast','Zero-based budget','Flexible budget','D','A flexible budget restates the cost allowance at the activity level actually achieved. That removes the volume effect so the remaining variances reflect price and efficiency.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Planning, Budgeting and Forecasting','hard','A regression of overhead on machine hours gives cost = 50,000 + 12x with an R-squared of 0.35. What is the most defensible conclusion?','Machine hours explain little of the variation, so a different or additional driver should be tested before relying on the model','Use the equation, treating the intercept as the fixed cost','The model proves overhead is variable','An R-squared above 0.30 is sufficient for budgeting','A','An R-squared of 0.35 means 65% of the variation is unexplained by the chosen driver. Relying on such a model to set budget allowances would embed large forecasting error, so alternative drivers should be tested first.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — External Financial Reporting Decisions','exam','A company changes from FIFO to weighted average cost in a period of rising prices. What is the immediate effect on reported gross profit, all else equal?','Gross profit rises because cost of sales falls','Gross profit falls because cost of sales moves toward more recent, higher costs','No effect, because inventory methods are presentational','Gross profit is unchanged but inventory rises','B','With rising prices FIFO charges older and cheaper costs to cost of sales, so weighted average charges more. A higher cost of sales means lower gross profit.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — External Financial Reporting Decisions','exam','A marketplace records the full contract value as revenue and the supplier settlement as an expense although it never controls the goods. What is the effect on the financial statements?','Net income is overstated','Only the balance sheet is affected','Revenue and expenses are both overstated while net income is unaffected','Cash flow from operations is overstated','C','Gross presentation by an agent inflates revenue and cost of sales by the same amount. Net income is unchanged, but scale, margin percentage and growth ratios are materially distorted.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Internal Controls','exam','A finance clerk can enter a supplier bank change and also release the payment run. Which control most directly addresses the resulting risk?','A monthly bank reconciliation','Stronger password rotation','An annual internal audit of payables','A dual-approval workflow in which a bank-detail change is confirmed by a second person using an independently sourced contact','D','The risk is fraudulent diversion of payments through an altered payee record. Call-back verification to an independently sourced contact plus a second approver stops one person both making and using the change.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Internal Controls','hard','Under the COSO framework a company has documented policies but no evidence they operate. Which conclusion is correct?','Control activity design is present but operating effectiveness is unproven, so the control cannot be relied on','The Control Environment deficiency is compensating','Only Monitoring is affected, and documentation is sufficient','Risk Assessment is satisfied because the policy list is the assessment','A','A control that exists on paper but leaves no evidence of operation fails the operating effectiveness test. Design and operating effectiveness are assessed separately and both are required before reliance.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Technology and Analytics','exam','An analyst finds that suppliers replying to a request for quotation within four hours win 62% of awards and concludes that fast replies cause awards. What is the strongest objection?','The sample is too recent','Correlation does not establish causation, since capable or better-fit suppliers may both reply faster and win','A rate of 62% is not statistically significant by definition','Response time cannot be measured reliably','B','Speed and win rate may share a common cause such as supplier capability or fit with the request. Establishing causation would require a controlled comparison rather than an observed association.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Technology and Analytics','exam','Which analytics type answers the question of what should be done about supplier churn rather than why it happened?','Descriptive','Diagnostic','Prescriptive','Predictive','C','Prescriptive analytics recommends an action, typically through optimisation or decision rules. Diagnostic analytics explains causes and predictive analytics estimates what happens next.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Financial Statement Analysis','exam','A company has current assets of 900,000 including inventory of 400,000 and prepaid expenses of 50,000, against current liabilities of 500,000. What is the quick ratio?','1.80','0.80','1.00','0.90','D','The quick ratio excludes inventory and prepayments: (900,000 - 400,000 - 50,000) / 500,000 = 0.90. The current ratio of 1.80 includes those less liquid items.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Financial Statement Analysis','hard','Return on equity rose from 12% to 18% while net profit margin and asset turnover were flat. Using DuPont analysis, what changed?','Financial leverage increased','Operating efficiency improved','The tax rate fell','Asset quality improved','A','DuPont decomposes ROE into margin, asset turnover and the equity multiplier. With the first two unchanged the rise must come from higher leverage, which raises both return and risk.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Financial Statement Analysis','exam','Days sales outstanding rose from 35 to 62 while revenue grew 20%. What is the most important operating consequence to flag to management?','Improved customer satisfaction','A working capital drain and a higher risk of bad debt that requires collection action','A reduction in the cost of capital','Lower inventory holding cost','B','Cash is being tied up in receivables faster than sales are growing, which consumes working capital. Ageing receivables also raise expected credit losses, so collections need attention.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Corporate Finance','exam','A firm has a cost of equity of 14%, a pre-tax cost of debt of 8%, a tax rate of 9% and a capital structure of 60% equity and 40% debt. What is the weighted average cost of capital?','8.40%','12.40%','11.31%','13.28%','C','The after-tax cost of debt is 8% x (1 - 0.09) = 7.28%. WACC is 0.6 x 14% + 0.4 x 7.28% = 8.4% + 2.91% = 11.31%.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Corporate Finance','exam','A supplier offers terms of 2/10 net 40. What is the approximate annualised cost of forgoing the discount?','2.0%','36.7%','18.0%','24.8%','D','Forgoing the discount costs 2/98 for thirty extra days of credit. Annualised, (2/98) x (365/30) is about 24.8%, far above normal borrowing rates.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Corporate Finance','hard','A company holds 3,000,000 of cash earning 1% while carrying a revolving facility drawn at 9%. Ignoring covenant restrictions, what is the most defensible treasury action?','Repay the revolver with surplus cash, retaining only the operating and buffer balance','Invest the cash in longer-dated securities to earn more','Distribute the cash as a dividend','Hold the cash, because liquidity always dominates','A','Paying down 9% debt with 1% cash earns an immediate risk-free spread of 8% on the amount repaid. A liquidity buffer is retained, but idle cash beyond it destroys value while a costlier facility is drawn.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Decision Analysis','exam','A product sells for 50, has a variable cost of 30 and is allocated 15 of fixed overhead that will not change if it is dropped. Should it be dropped?','Yes, because it loses 5 per unit','No, because it contributes 20 per unit toward fixed costs','Yes, if volume is below breakeven','Only if the allocation base changes','B','Allocated fixed cost that does not change is not relevant to the drop decision. The product contributes 50 - 30 = 20 per unit, so dropping it reduces total profit.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Decision Analysis','exam','A machine bought for 400,000 has a carrying amount of 150,000 and a trade-in value of 90,000 against a replacement. In the replace-or-keep analysis, which amount is irrelevant?','The 90,000 trade-in value','The future operating savings','The 150,000 carrying amount, which is a sunk cost','The purchase price of the new machine','C','The carrying amount reflects past spending that the decision cannot change. Trade-in proceeds, the new machine cost and future savings are all future cash flows that differ between the alternatives.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Decision Analysis','hard','Machine time is the binding constraint. Product X contributes 40 per unit and uses 4 machine hours; product Y contributes 25 and uses 2 hours. Which is prioritised and why?','X, because its contribution per unit is higher','Neither, because priority follows sales price','X, because it uses more of the scarce resource','Y, because its contribution per machine hour is 12.50 against 10.00 for X','D','With a binding constraint the objective is contribution per unit of the constraint, not per unit of product. Y yields 12.50 per machine hour against 10.00 for X, so Y is produced first.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Investment Decisions','exam','A project costs 500,000 and returns 150,000 a year for five years. At a 10% discount rate the five-year annuity factor is 3.7908. What is the net present value?','68,620','-500,000','250,000','168,620','A','The present value of the inflows is 150,000 x 3.7908 = 568,620. Subtracting the 500,000 outlay gives an NPV of 68,620, so the project adds value.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Investment Decisions','hard','Two mutually exclusive projects have the same NPV at the cost of capital but different internal rates of return and cash flow timing. Which criterion should decide and why?','IRR, because a percentage return is comparable across projects','NPV, because it measures absolute value added and does not assume reinvestment at the IRR','Payback, because it controls risk','Profitability index, because it ranks by size','B','IRR implicitly assumes intermediate cash flows are reinvested at the IRR, which is unrealistic for mutually exclusive projects with different timing. NPV measures the absolute increase in value at the true cost of capital.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Investment Decisions','exam','A project has a payback period of 2.5 years against a 3-year cut-off but a negative NPV. What is the correct recommendation?','Accept, because payback is the primary screen','Accept if the IRR is positive','Reject, because payback ignores post-cut-off cash flows and the time value of money while NPV is negative','Defer until payback falls below two years','C','Payback is a liquidity screen rather than a value measure and ignores both discounting and later cash flows. A negative NPV means the project destroys value and should be rejected.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Risk Management','exam','A company earns fees in USD, pays most costs in AED which is pegged to USD, and has one large EUR-denominated supplier contract. Where is the material FX exposure?','The USD fee revenue','There is no exposure because of the peg','The AED cost base','The EUR supplier contract','D','The AED-USD peg removes most transaction and translation risk between those currencies. The unhedged EUR obligation is the exposure that can move against the company.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Risk Management','hard','A risk register scores supplier concentration at likelihood 2 and impact 5, and data entry error at likelihood 5 and impact 1. What is the flaw in ranking purely by the product of the two?','A low-likelihood, high-impact risk can threaten survival while a frequent low-impact risk cannot, so severity must be considered separately','Nothing, since the product is the standard measure','Likelihood should always be weighted double','The scale should have ten points','A','Multiplying likelihood by impact hides the difference between a nuisance and an existential event. Risk appetite statements normally treat high-impact tail risks separately regardless of their expected value score.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Professional Ethics','exam','A management accountant is instructed by a superior to defer recognising a known expense so a bonus target is met. Under the IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice, what is the first step?','Resign immediately','Discuss the issue with the immediate supervisor unless that person is involved, then escalate to the next level','Report externally to the regulator','Comply, because the superior carries the responsibility','B','The IMA resolution process begins with internal escalation, starting with the immediate superior unless that person is implicated and then the next management level. External disclosure is a last resort once internal avenues are exhausted.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Professional Ethics','exam','Which IMA overarching ethical principle is most directly breached by presenting a favourable forecast while withholding a known contract cancellation?','Competence','Confidentiality','Credibility, through failure to communicate information fairly and objectively','Independence of the external auditor','C','Credibility requires communicating information fairly and objectively and disclosing all information that could reasonably influence a user understanding. Withholding a known cancellation misleads the reader.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Cost Management','exam','A service company using a single plant-wide overhead rate finds that low-volume, complex accounts appear highly profitable. What distortion is most likely?','Overcosting of complex accounts','Overcosting of simple accounts only','No distortion, because overhead is immaterial','Undercosting of complex accounts, because a volume-based rate pushes cost toward high-volume simple accounts','D','Volume-based allocation assigns cost in proportion to volume, so complex low-volume work that consumes disproportionate support is undercosted. High-volume simple work absorbs the cost instead and looks less profitable than it is.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Performance Management','exam','Two divisions transfer an intermediate service. The selling division has spare capacity and a variable cost of 40; the external market price is 70. What transfer price range keeps both divisions willing to trade?','Between 40 and the price the buying division would pay externally','Exactly 70','Exactly 40','Any price above 70','A','With spare capacity the selling division opportunity cost is only its variable cost of 40, which sets the floor. The ceiling is the buyer external purchase price, so any price in between benefits both divisions.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Planning, Budgeting and Forecasting','exam','A company adopts a rolling twelve-month forecast updated monthly instead of an annual budget locked in October. What is the main benefit?','It removes the need for variance analysis','The planning horizon stays constant and the plan absorbs new information continuously','It reduces the number of cost centres','It guarantees a favourable volume variance','B','A rolling forecast always looks the same distance ahead and incorporates the latest actuals each cycle. Resource decisions therefore stay current instead of anchored to assumptions made once a year.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Financial Statement Analysis','exam','A company capitalises development costs that a competitor expenses. Comparing the two on EBITDA alone would','be reliable, because EBITDA excludes amortisation','understate the capitalising company','overstate the capitalising company, because the capitalised spend leaves operating cost and its amortisation is added back','have no effect on comparability','C','Capitalising moves current spend out of operating expense, and EBITDA then also excludes the resulting amortisation. The capitalising firm reports higher EBITDA for identical economics, so free cash flow is the fairer comparison.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Corporate Finance','exam','A company must fund a seasonal working capital swing lasting four months each year. Which financing is most appropriate?','A ten-year amortising term loan','A finance lease over the assets','An equity issue','A revolving credit facility drawn and repaid within the cycle','D','Short-lived, self-liquidating asset needs are matched with short-term revolving finance. Long-term debt or equity would leave the company paying for capital it does not need for eight months of the year.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Internal Controls','exam','A payment run of 40 invoices includes one to a supplier created the same day for an amount just below the second-approval threshold. Which test detects this pattern systematically?','A continuous monitoring rule flagging payments to newly created suppliers and amounts clustered just below approval thresholds','Sampling ten invoices at random each month','An annual supplier satisfaction survey','A quarterly bank reconciliation','A','The pattern is a known fraud signature combining a new payee with deliberate threshold avoidance. A rules-based continuous monitoring routine tests the whole population rather than a small random sample.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 1 — Technology and Analytics','hard','A model predicts supplier default with 97% accuracy in a population where 3% actually default. Why is accuracy the wrong headline metric?','It is not; 97% is excellent','A model predicting no default for everyone scores the same 97%, so recall, precision and the cost of each error type must be reported','Accuracy is only valid above 99%','The sample must exceed 10,000 records','B','With a heavily imbalanced class a trivial constant prediction achieves the base rate. Recall on the minority class and the asymmetric cost of a missed default determine whether the model is useful.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Risk Management','exam','A company buys business interruption insurance for a low-probability, high-impact outage. In risk response terms this is','risk avoidance','risk acceptance','risk transfer','risk reduction','C','Insurance shifts the financial consequence of the event to a third party without changing its likelihood. Avoidance would stop the activity and reduction would lower likelihood or impact directly.'),
('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)','Part 2 — Decision Analysis','exam','A firm considers a one-off export order at 35 per unit when full absorption cost is 42 and variable cost is 28, with spare capacity and no effect on existing sales. What is the correct decision?','Reject, because the price is below full cost','Accept only if the customer pays in advance','Reject unless the price covers 42','Accept, because each unit adds 7 of contribution while fixed costs are unchanged','D','With spare capacity and no cannibalisation only incremental costs are relevant. The order adds 35 - 28 = 7 per unit of contribution and therefore increases profit.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CMA (Certified Management Accountant)');

-- ============================================================================
-- ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants) — ACCA
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Accounting for transactions','exam','An entity sells goods with a right of return. Revenue of 500,000 has been billed and the entity expects 6% of goods to be returned. How is revenue recognised?','470,000, with a refund liability of 30,000 and an asset for the right to recover the goods','500,000, with a provision for returns in expenses','500,000, adjusted only when returns actually occur','Nothing until the return period expires','A','Under IFRS 15 revenue is recognised only for the consideration to which the entity expects to be entitled, so the expected returns are excluded. A refund liability is recognised together with an asset for the right to recover the goods at their former carrying amount.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Accounting for transactions','exam','A platform charges suppliers a 3% success fee. In the year it billed AED 900,000 of fees, of which AED 120,000 relates to introductions where the supplier and buyer had not yet contracted at the reporting date. What revenue is recognised?','AED 900,000','AED 780,000, with AED 120,000 as a contract liability','AED 120,000','Nothing until cash is received','B','The performance obligation is satisfied when the introduction results in a contract that triggers the fee. Amounts billed before that point are contract liabilities, so only AED 780,000 is revenue.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Non-current assets','exam','An asset with a carrying amount of 800,000 has a fair value less costs of disposal of 700,000 and a value in use of 760,000. What impairment loss is recognised?','100,000','160,000','40,000','No impairment','C','The recoverable amount is the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use, which is 760,000. The impairment is 800,000 - 760,000 = 40,000.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Non-current assets','hard','Development expenditure of 400,000 was incurred, of which 150,000 was spent before the entity could demonstrate technical feasibility and intention to complete. How much is capitalised?','400,000','Nothing, as internally generated intangibles cannot be capitalised','150,000','250,000','D','IAS 38 permits capitalisation only from the date all six development criteria are met; earlier spend is research-like and expensed. Expenditure already expensed cannot be reinstated, so 250,000 is capitalised.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Financial instruments','exam','An entity holds trade receivables it intends to collect, with cash flows that are solely principal and interest. Under IFRS 9 how are they measured?','Amortised cost, with a loss allowance for expected credit losses','Fair value through profit or loss','Fair value through other comprehensive income with recycling','At the invoice amount with no allowance until default occurs','A','A hold-to-collect business model with cash flows that are solely payments of principal and interest gives amortised cost measurement. IFRS 9 requires an expected credit loss allowance rather than waiting for a default event.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Financial instruments','hard','A company issues a convertible bond of 5,000,000 with a 4% coupon when the market rate for similar non-convertible debt is 7%. How is the instrument presented?','Entirely as a financial liability','Split, with the present value of the cash flows at 7% as a liability and the residual in equity','Entirely as equity because conversion is expected','As a provision until conversion','B','A convertible bond is a compound instrument under IAS 32. The liability is measured first as the present value of the contractual cash flows discounted at the market rate for similar debt without the conversion feature, and the residual is the equity component.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Preparation of financial statements','exam','A subsidiary sold goods to its parent for 200,000 at a 25% mark-up on cost, and half remain in the parent inventory at the year end. What consolidation adjustment is required for the unrealised profit?','Eliminate 40,000','Eliminate 50,000','Eliminate 20,000','No adjustment, as the sale was at arm length','C','Profit on the full sale is 200,000 x 25/125 = 40,000, and half of the goods remain in group inventory. The unrealised element of 20,000 is eliminated from inventory and group profit.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Preparation of financial statements','exam','A company includes the cash paid to acquire a subsidiary, net of cash acquired, in its statement of cash flows. In which section does it belong?','Operating activities','Not presented, only disclosed','Financing activities','Investing activities','D','Payments to acquire subsidiaries and other businesses are investing cash flows under IAS 7. The amount is presented net of any cash and cash equivalents acquired.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AA — Audit evidence','exam','An auditor attends an inventory count and observes that the client counts only items on the system listing rather than the whole warehouse. What assertion is most at risk?','Completeness','Existence','Valuation','Rights and obligations','A','Counting only what the system already records cannot reveal inventory the system omits. That is a completeness risk, whereas testing from the floor to the listing would address it.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AA — Audit evidence','exam','Which source of audit evidence is generally the most reliable?','A verbal explanation from the finance director','A bank confirmation received directly by the auditor from the bank','A photocopy of a supplier invoice supplied by the client','A management-prepared reconciliation','B','Evidence obtained directly by the auditor from an independent external source is the most reliable. Client-generated or verbal evidence is weaker because it is not independent of management.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AA — Planning and risk assessment','hard','The auditor identifies that a client recognises platform fees on a manual journal outside the billing system at each month end. What is the appropriate response?','Rely on the control because the journal is reviewed','Reduce sample sizes because the journals are few','Treat manual revenue journals as a fraud risk, test the complete population of such journals and agree them to underlying transactions','Request that the client stop making the journals','C','Manual journals to revenue are a classic management override route and ISA 240 presumes a fraud risk in revenue recognition. The response is to test the population of such entries against evidence of the underlying transactions.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AA — Review and reporting','exam','After completing the audit the auditor concludes there is a material uncertainty related to going concern, and the disclosure in the notes is adequate. What is the reporting outcome?','Qualified opinion','Disclaimer of opinion','Adverse opinion','Unmodified opinion with a separate section headed Material Uncertainty Related to Going Concern','D','If the disclosure is adequate the opinion is not modified, but the auditor draws attention to the uncertainty in a dedicated section. Modification follows only where the disclosure is inadequate.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AA — Internal control','exam','A company allows the same person to record cash receipts and prepare the bank reconciliation. Which control objective fails and what is the remedy?','Segregation of duties; have the reconciliation prepared or reviewed by someone independent of cash recording','Authorisation; introduce a payment limit','Physical control; use a safe','Documentation; retain remittance advices longer','A','If the recorder also reconciles, a misappropriation can be concealed in the reconciliation. Independent preparation or review restores the detective control.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AA — Professional ethics','exam','An audit firm derives 22% of its total fee income from one listed client for two consecutive years. What threat arises and what is required?','Advocacy threat; obtain client consent','Self-interest threat from fee dependency; disclose to those charged with governance and arrange a pre-issuance or post-issuance review','Familiarity threat; rotate the audit junior','No threat, as the limit is 30%','B','Fee dependency above 15% of total fees for a public interest entity creates a self-interest threat. The Code requires disclosure to those charged with governance and a review by an external professional accountant.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FM — Working capital management','exam','Inventory days are 60, receivable days are 45 and payable days are 30. What is the cash operating cycle?','135 days','90 days','75 days','15 days','C','The cash operating cycle is inventory days plus receivable days less payable days: 60 + 45 - 30 = 75. It measures how long cash is tied up between paying suppliers and collecting from customers.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FM — Working capital management','hard','A company with a cash operating cycle of 75 days and annual cost of sales of 7,300,000 wants to release cash by cutting receivable days from 45 to 30. Approximately how much cash is released, using cost of sales as the base?','75,000','150,000','1,000,000','300,000','D','Each day of the cycle ties up roughly 7,300,000 / 365 = 20,000. Removing fifteen days releases about 15 x 20,000 = 300,000 of working capital.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FM — Investment appraisal','exam','A project has an NPV of 120,000 at 10% and an NPV of -30,000 at 15%. What is the approximate internal rate of return by linear interpolation?','14.0%','12.0%','15.0%','11.5%','A','Interpolating gives 10% + (120,000 / 150,000) x 5% = 14.0%. The IRR is the discount rate at which NPV is zero, between the two tested rates.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FM — Investment appraisal','exam','Inflation is expected at 5% and the money cost of capital is 12%. What real discount rate should be applied to cash flows expressed in current prices?','7.0%','6.7%','17.6%','5.0%','B','The Fisher relation gives (1.12 / 1.05) - 1 = 6.67%. Real cash flows must be discounted at the real rate, and money cash flows at the money rate.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FM — Business finance','exam','A company with volatile cash flows and few tangible assets is choosing between a bank term loan and an equity issue. Which factor most favours equity?','Interest is tax deductible','Equity is always cheaper than debt','Volatile cash flows make fixed servicing commitments risky and the asset base offers little security','Equity avoids dilution of control','C','Debt requires fixed servicing regardless of trading, which raises financial risk where cash flows are volatile and there is little collateral. Equity has no contractual repayment, though it is more expensive and dilutes control.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FM — Risk management','exam','A UAE company owes EUR 500,000 in three months and fears the euro strengthening. Which hedge fixes the AED cost?','Sell euros forward','Buy a euro put option','Do nothing because the dirham is pegged','Buy euros forward for the settlement date','D','The exposure is a future euro payment, so the risk is the euro rising. Buying euros forward fixes the rate today; the peg is to the US dollar and does not protect against euro movements.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','TX — Value added tax','exam','A UAE business made taxable supplies of AED 360,000 in the past twelve months and expects AED 500,000 in the next thirty days from a signed contract. What is its position?','Mandatory registration is triggered because taxable supplies are expected to exceed AED 375,000 within the next thirty days','No registration is required until supplies are actually made','Only voluntary registration is available','Registration is never required for services','A','The mandatory VAT registration test is met either by exceeding AED 375,000 in the previous twelve months or by expecting to exceed it within the next thirty days. The anticipated contract triggers registration.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','TX — Value added tax','exam','A registered UAE business issues an invoice for AED 105,000 including VAT at the standard rate. What is the output VAT?','AED 5,250','AED 5,000','AED 5,500','AED 0','B','The amount is VAT-inclusive, so the tax fraction is 5/105. AED 105,000 x 5/105 = AED 5,000 of output VAT on a net supply of AED 100,000.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','TX — Corporate taxation','hard','A UAE-resident company has accounting profit of AED 700,000 including AED 50,000 of non-deductible entertainment-type expenditure that is only half allowed. What is the Corporate Tax charge, ignoring Small Business Relief?','AED 29,250','AED 65,250','AED 31,500','AED 26,550','C','Taxable income is the accounting profit of 700,000 plus the 25,000 disallowed half of the expenditure, giving 725,000. Tax is (725,000 - 375,000) x 9% = AED 31,500.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','SBR — Group accounting','exam','A parent acquires a further 10% of an already controlled subsidiary from the non-controlling interest for more than the carrying amount of that interest. How is the difference treated?','As goodwill','As a remeasurement of the subsidiary net assets','As a loss in profit or loss','As a transaction with owners, adjusted directly in equity','D','Changes in ownership that do not result in a loss of control are transactions between equity holders. The difference between consideration and the carrying amount of the non-controlling interest is recognised in equity.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','SBR — Group accounting','hard','An investor holds 40% of an entity and can appoint three of seven board members, with no other party holding more than 15%. How is the investment most likely accounted for?','As an associate, using the equity method, unless the facts show de facto control','As a subsidiary, with full consolidation','At fair value through profit or loss','As a joint operation','A','A 40% holding with board representation gives significant influence, which is presumed at 20% or more. Consolidation would require control, and the facts as stated show influence rather than the power to direct relevant activities alone.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','SBR — Ethical and professional issues','exam','A finance director asks the reporting accountant to classify a recurring operating cost as an exceptional item to improve the underlying profit measure. What is the correct response?','Comply, since alternative performance measures are unregulated','Decline, because presenting a recurring cost as exceptional misleads users and breaches the objectivity and integrity principles','Comply if the amount is disclosed in a note','Escalate only if the amount is material to the balance sheet','B','Labelling a recurring cost as exceptional distorts the underlying earnings measure that users rely on. The fundamental principles of integrity and objectivity require the accountant to refuse and, if pressed, escalate.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','PM — Decision-making techniques','exam','A company faces a limiting factor of skilled labour hours. Which technique identifies the optimal production mix across several products and constraints?','Absorption costing','Life cycle costing','Linear programming','Payback analysis','C','Linear programming maximises an objective function such as total contribution subject to several resource constraints. Costing methods allocate cost and do not solve the allocation of a scarce resource.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','PM — Performance measurement','exam','A divisional manager is measured only on annual profit and cuts staff training and preventive maintenance in the final quarter. What weakness does this show?','The measure is not quantitative enough','Poor variance analysis technique','Excessive delegation of authority','Short-termism, because a single financial measure ignores the long-run drivers of performance','D','A single short-horizon financial measure can be improved by deferring discretionary spend that protects future performance. A balanced set of measures covering customer, process and learning perspectives counteracts this.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','PM — Budgeting and control','exam','A company uses incremental budgeting and finds a legacy contract cost has been rolled forward for six years without review. Which approach directly addresses this?','Zero-based budgeting, which requires each activity to be justified from a nil base','Flexible budgeting','Rolling budgets','Activity-based costing','A','Incremental budgeting perpetuates existing spend by starting from last year. Zero-based budgeting forces each item to be justified afresh, exposing costs that no longer earn their place.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','MA — Cost accounting','exam','Fixed costs are 240,000, selling price is 60 and variable cost is 36. What sales volume achieves a target profit of 120,000?','10,000 units','15,000 units','6,000 units','5,000 units','B','Contribution per unit is 60 - 36 = 24. Required volume is (240,000 + 120,000) / 24 = 15,000 units.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','MA — Cost accounting','exam','Using the same data, what is the margin of safety in units if budgeted sales are 15,000?','15,000 units','10,000 units','5,000 units','No margin of safety','C','Breakeven volume is 240,000 / 24 = 10,000 units. The margin of safety is 15,000 - 10,000 = 5,000 units, or one third of budgeted sales.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FA — Recording transactions and events','exam','A company debits a repair to the machinery cost account instead of repairs expense. What is the effect on the financial statements before correction?','Profit understated and assets understated','Profit overstated and liabilities overstated','No effect, because the trial balance still balances','Profit overstated and assets overstated','D','Capitalising a revenue expense removes it from the income statement and inflates the asset. Profit and non-current assets are both overstated until the error is corrected.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FA — Preparing basic financial statements','exam','A suspense account of 900 credit arises because a receipt of 450 from a customer was posted to the wrong side of the receivables account. What is the correcting entry?','Debit receivables 900, credit suspense 900','Debit receivables 450, credit suspense 450','Debit suspense 450, credit receivables 450','Debit suspense 900, credit receivables 900','A','Posting a credit as a debit, or the reverse, produces an error of twice the amount. The correction is 2 x 450 = 900 debited to receivables against the suspense account.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','LW — Employment and company law','exam','A director of a private company personally takes a contract opportunity that the company was negotiating. Which duty is breached?','The duty to exercise reasonable care and skill','The duty to avoid conflicts of interest and not to exploit corporate opportunity','The duty to keep accounting records','The duty to file accounts on time','B','Diverting a maturing corporate opportunity to personal benefit breaches the conflict of interest duty. The company can require the director to account for the profit made.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AB — Governance and internal control','exam','A board has an audit committee composed of two executive directors and one independent non-executive. What is the governance weakness?','The committee is too large','There is no weakness if the finance director chairs it','The committee should consist wholly of independent non-executive directors to review management objectively','The committee should include the external auditor as a member','C','An audit committee exists to oversee management, so members who are part of management cannot review it independently. Codes require the committee to be composed of independent non-executive directors.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','AA — Planning and risk assessment','exam','Audit risk consists of which components?','Detection risk and business risk','Fraud risk and going concern risk','Sampling risk and materiality','Inherent risk, control risk and detection risk','D','Audit risk is the risk of an inappropriate opinion and combines the risk of material misstatement, made up of inherent and control risk, with detection risk. The auditor controls only detection risk, by the nature, timing and extent of procedures.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FR — Leases and provisions','exam','A company faces a legal claim it will probably lose, with the best estimate of the cost between 200,000 and 300,000 and no point in the range more likely than another. What is provided?','250,000, the mid-point of the range','Nothing until judgment is given','200,000, the lowest amount','300,000, the highest amount','A','IAS 37 requires the best estimate of the expenditure required to settle the obligation. Where a continuous range exists and no outcome is more likely, the mid-point is used.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','FM — Business valuations','hard','A company has sustainable earnings of 800,000 and comparable listed companies trade on a price-earnings ratio of 12. What is a reasonable equity value for this unlisted company?','9,600,000 with no adjustment','Below 9,600,000, after a discount for the lack of marketability and the smaller size of an unlisted company','Above 9,600,000, because private companies are scarcer','Cannot be estimated without the asset base','B','Applying a listed multiple gives 9,600,000 as a starting point. Unlisted shares are less marketable and typically carry higher risk, so a discount is applied to the listed multiple.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','SBR — Ethical and professional issues','hard','An accountant discovers a suspected money laundering transaction while preparing a client account. What is the required action under anti-money laundering obligations?','Confront the client and ask for an explanation','Resign quietly and take no further action','Report the suspicion internally to the money laundering reporting officer without alerting the client','Adjust the accounts to remove the transaction','C','Suspicion must be reported to the nominated officer through the internal channel. Telling the client risks committing the separate offence of tipping off.'),
('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)','PM — Performance measurement','hard','A marketplace reports 81 suppliers invited and 0 registered. Which measure best diagnoses the failure rather than restating it?','Total invitations sent per month','Average invitation cost','Cumulative supplier count since launch','Conversion rate at each stage of the registration funnel, with the drop-off point identified','D','A single zero states the outcome without locating the cause. Stage-by-stage conversion shows exactly where candidates leave the funnel, which is what a corrective action needs.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)');

-- ============================================================================
-- APS (Accredited Payables Specialist) — IOFM
-- ============================================================================
insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Invoice Processing','exam','An invoice for 9,800 arrives against a purchase order of 10,000 with goods received of 9,600. Under three-way matching with a 2% tolerance, what should happen?','Hold the invoice, because the quantity received is less than the quantity billed and the shortfall must be resolved','Pay in full, since the invoice is below the purchase order','Pay 10,000 to match the purchase order','Cancel the purchase order','A','Three-way matching compares purchase order, receipt and invoice; the binding constraint is what was actually received. Billing above receipt is an exception to resolve with receiving or the supplier, not a tolerance to absorb.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Invoice Processing','exam','A supplier submits an invoice with no purchase order number for a service already delivered. What is the best practice response?','Pay it to preserve the relationship','Route it through the non-purchase-order approval workflow to the budget owner who can attest to receipt','Reject it permanently','Post it to a suspense account and pay at year end','B','Services often lack a receipt document, so the control substitute is an attestation from the person with budget authority who can confirm delivery. Paying without any approval removes the control entirely.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Invoice Processing','hard','An analysis shows 18% of invoices require manual intervention, most because the purchase order number is missing or wrong. Which intervention removes the most rework at the source?','Hire additional processors','Increase the matching tolerance to 10%','Enforce purchase order issuance and print the number on the order so suppliers quote it, and reject supplier submissions that omit it through the portal','Move to monthly rather than weekly payment runs','C','Exceptions are cheapest to remove where they are created, which is at supplier submission. Adding staff or widening tolerances treats the symptom and widens control risk respectively.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Invoice Processing','exam','A supplier sends the same invoice by email and through the portal. Which control prevents a duplicate payment?','A monthly supplier statement reconciliation only','Requiring two approvers on all invoices','Paying only from original paper documents','A system duplicate check on supplier, invoice number, amount and date at the point of entry','D','The reliable prevention is an automated duplicate test on the key fields at entry, before an obligation is created. Statement reconciliation is detective and finds the duplicate only after payment.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Vendor Master File Management','exam','A request arrives by email from a known supplier contact asking to change bank details before the next payment run. What is the correct process?','Verify by call-back to a telephone number held in the master file from before the request, then apply the change under dual control','Update the record, since the sender address is recognised','Update it and note the email in the file','Ask the requester to confirm by a second email','A','Business email compromise relies on the request itself supplying the verification channel. Call-back to a previously held number, plus a second person applying the change, breaks that dependency.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Vendor Master File Management','exam','A vendor master file audit finds two records for the same legal entity with different bank accounts. What is the primary risk?','Higher storage cost','Duplicate payment and a concealed fraudulent payee record','Slower reporting','Loss of early payment discounts','B','Duplicate vendor records let the same invoice be paid twice under different identifiers and can hide a fraudulent account. De-duplication with a single active record and a documented merge is the remedy.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Vendor Master File Management','hard','Which combination of vendor master controls most reduces the risk of a fictitious supplier being paid?','A quarterly review of inactive vendors and an annual audit','Requiring purchase orders on all spend','Segregation between vendor creation and payment approval, evidence of legal existence such as a trade licence at onboarding, and periodic screening of vendor bank and address data against employee records','Restricting payments to bank transfer only','C','A fictitious supplier needs a record that someone can create and then pay. Splitting those duties, verifying legal existence at onboarding and matching vendor details against employee data attacks each step of the scheme.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Vendor Master File Management','exam','A UAE supplier is onboarded and provides a tax registration number. What should accounts payable verify before recovering input VAT on its invoices?','Only that the number is fifteen digits','That the invoice is under AED 375,000','That the supplier bank is in the UAE','That the supplier is genuinely registered and the invoice meets the tax invoice content requirements','D','Input VAT recovery depends on holding a valid tax invoice from a registered supplier. Checking the registration and the required invoice content protects the recovery from being denied.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Payments and Fraud Prevention','exam','Which payment method carries the lowest inherent fraud risk when properly controlled?','Electronic transfer released under dual authorisation with positive payee verification','Cheque','Cash advance','Virtual card issued without spend limits','A','Electronic payments under dual release and payee verification create an auditable, controlled path. Cheques are exposed to alteration and interception, and uncapped cards remove the spend limit control.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Payments and Fraud Prevention','exam','An urgent same-day payment is requested by a senior executive by message, bypassing the normal run and approval. What should the payables specialist do?','Process it, because the executive has authority','Follow the exception procedure, verify the request through a known channel independent of the message and obtain documented approval','Process it and obtain approval afterwards','Refuse without escalation','B','Urgency and authority pressure are the standard features of a business email compromise attempt. The control is out-of-band verification and documented approval before, not after, the funds move.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Payments and Fraud Prevention','hard','A company uses positive pay for cheques. What exposure does positive pay leave unaddressed?','Altered payee names on presented cheques','Duplicate cheque numbers','Payments to a legitimate but fraudulently created vendor record, since the cheque itself matches the issued file','Cheques presented after the stale date','C','Positive pay compares presented items to the issued file and catches alteration and forgery. It cannot detect a payment that was properly issued to a payee that should never have existed.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Payments and Fraud Prevention','exam','Bank reconciliation shows a payment of 46,500 cleared against a recorded amount of 4,650. What is the most likely cause and the first action?','A supplier overcharge; contact the supplier','A timing difference; carry it forward','A bank fee; post the difference','A transposition or decimal keying error; contact the bank to recall and correct the entry, and investigate how it passed approval','D','A tenfold difference points to a keying error rather than a commercial dispute. Recovery is urgent, and the control question is how a value error passed the approval and release steps.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Internal Controls and Audit','exam','Which duties must be segregated in a payables function?','Vendor master maintenance, invoice approval and payment release','Invoice entry and invoice scanning','Filing and archiving','Statement reconciliation and month-end reporting','A','Anyone able to create a payee, approve an invoice to it and release the payment can complete a fraud alone. Those three are the incompatible duties that must sit with different people.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Internal Controls and Audit','exam','An auditor asks for evidence that the payables approval matrix operates. What is the strongest evidence?','The written policy document','System-generated approval records for a sample of payments showing the approver, amount and timestamp against the matrix','A memo from the payables manager','The list of users with approval rights','B','A policy and a rights list evidence design, not operation. Approval records tied to specific payments demonstrate that the control actually ran on real transactions.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Internal Controls and Audit','hard','Testing finds that 12 of 40 sampled invoices were approved by the same person who entered them, in an emergency period when a colleague was absent. What is the correct conclusion?','The control is effective because the circumstances were exceptional','No conclusion is possible without a larger sample','The control failed for those items; the exception rate must be projected, the compensating review evaluated, and a documented delegation process put in place','The control is effective if no loss occurred','C','A control that stops operating under staffing pressure has failed for the period, whatever the reason and whether or not a loss followed. The response is to assess the exception rate, look for compensating detection and formalise delegation.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Accounting and Month-End Close','exam','Goods worth 80,000 were received on 28 December and the invoice arrives on 6 January. What entry is required at 31 December?','Nothing, because no invoice exists','Record it in January only','Recognise a prepayment of 80,000','Accrue 80,000 to goods received not invoiced with the matching expense or asset','D','The obligation arises on receipt, not on invoicing. A goods-received-not-invoiced accrual records the liability in the correct period and reverses when the invoice is booked.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Accounting and Month-End Close','exam','The goods received not invoiced account contains items over 180 days old with no matching invoice. What does this most likely indicate?','Receipts recorded in error, cancelled orders or invoices posted without matching, all requiring investigation and clearance','Suppliers are paying too quickly','A tax exposure only','That the accrual should simply be written off to income','A','A stale balance in that account means the receipt and invoice sides never met, which distorts both liabilities and cost. Each aged item is investigated and either matched, corrected or released with documented approval.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Accounting and Month-End Close','exam','A supplier statement shows a balance of 210,000 while the ledger shows 185,000. Which reconciling item would explain the difference legitimately?','A duplicate payment made by the company','Invoices issued by the supplier that the company has not yet received or recorded, plus goods in transit','A supplier discount already taken','A currency gain on settlement','B','Timing differences in invoice receipt and goods in transit routinely make the supplier statement higher than the ledger. The reconciliation lists and ages each such item so genuine omissions are caught.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Tax and Regulatory Compliance','exam','A UAE supplier invoice shows AED 20,000 plus 5% VAT for a cost used entirely in making taxable supplies. What does payables record?','AED 21,000 of expense','AED 21,000 of expense and AED 1,000 of input VAT','AED 20,000 of expense and AED 1,000 of recoverable input VAT','AED 20,000 of expense only, ignoring the VAT','C','Recoverable input VAT is an asset and not a cost of the purchase. The expense is the net AED 20,000 and the AED 1,000 is claimed on the VAT return for the period.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Tax and Regulatory Compliance','exam','A supplier invoice for entertainment of employees carries 5% VAT. What is the general UAE treatment of that input tax?','Fully recoverable','Zero-rated so no VAT arises','Recoverable at half rate','Blocked from recovery, so the VAT becomes part of the cost','D','Input tax on entertainment and certain employee-related supplies is specifically blocked from recovery. Where recovery is blocked, the VAT is not an asset and forms part of the expense.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Tax and Regulatory Compliance','hard','An unregistered supplier issues a document labelled tax invoice showing 5% VAT. What should payables do?','Refuse to pay the VAT element, request a corrected invoice, and not claim any input tax','Pay it and recover the VAT','Pay it and expense the VAT','Report the supplier to the customer','A','Only a registered supplier may charge VAT, and only a valid tax invoice supports recovery. Paying and claiming the tax would expose the company to assessment and penalties.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Travel and Entertainment Expense','exam','An employee submits a claim for a meal with no receipt, stating the receipt was lost, for an amount above the policy de minimis. What is the correct handling?','Reimburse, since the employee is trusted','Decline until an acceptable substitute record and a manager attestation are provided as the policy requires','Reimburse half the amount','Reimburse and note it as an exception without approval','B','Above the de minimis the policy requires documentary support because the deduction and the control both depend on it. A documented substitute plus manager attestation is the standard exception route.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Travel and Entertainment Expense','exam','Analysis shows repeated claims from one employee just under the receipt threshold. What does this pattern suggest and what is the response?','Efficient expense management; no action','A system rounding error; adjust the threshold','Possible structuring to avoid documentation; run a pattern analysis over the employee history and refer to the policy owner','A currency conversion issue; recalculate','C','Amounts clustering just below a control threshold is a classic avoidance signature rather than a coincidence. The response is analytical review over time and referral, not a change to the threshold.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Automation and AP Technology','exam','A company implements optical character recognition for invoice capture. What determines whether the touchless rate actually improves?','The number of scanners installed','The number of approvers','The colour depth of the scan','The quality of vendor master and purchase order data that the captured fields must match against','D','Capture only removes typing; the invoice still has to match a supplier and an order automatically. Poor master data leaves the same exceptions and the touchless rate does not move.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Automation and AP Technology','hard','After automation, exception volumes fall but the days payable outstanding worsens. What is the most likely explanation to investigate first?','Invoices are now entered faster with earlier due dates recognised, or approval queues have become the new bottleneck, so cycle time must be measured by stage','Suppliers changed their terms simultaneously','The bank changed its settlement window','The automation reduced invoice volume','A','A metric that worsens after a throughput improvement usually points to a bottleneck moving rather than disappearing. Measuring cycle time by stage locates whether approval, not capture, now holds the invoice.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Automation and AP Technology','exam','What is the main financial benefit of straight-through processing for invoices that match cleanly?','It removes the need for a vendor master file','It shortens the cycle so early payment discounts become capturable and processing cost per invoice falls','It removes the need for approvals','It eliminates the need for accruals at period end','B','Discounts are lost mainly because invoices sit unprocessed past the discount window. Straight-through processing shortens the cycle and cuts the cost of each clean invoice.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Payments and Fraud Prevention','exam','A supplier offers terms of 2/10 net 30 and the company has surplus cash earning 2% a year. Should it take the discount?','No, because cash should be preserved','No, unless the supplier is strategic','Yes, because the annualised return from taking the discount is far above the 2% earned on cash','Only if the invoice exceeds 100,000','C','Taking a 2% discount twenty days early is an annualised return of roughly 37%. Any return that far above the 2% alternative use of cash justifies paying early.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Escheatment and Unclaimed Property','exam','Several supplier cheques issued eighteen months ago remain uncashed. What is the correct treatment?','Write them back to income immediately','Leave them outstanding indefinitely','Void them and reissue automatically','Attempt contact with the payee, keep documented due diligence records, and follow the applicable unclaimed property rules before any write-back','D','Uncashed payments remain the property of the payee until the applicable rules permit otherwise. Documented due diligence and compliance with the unclaimed property regime must precede any release to income.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Escheatment and Unclaimed Property','hard','A company routinely writes back credit balances on supplier accounts to income after twelve months without contacting the suppliers. What is the exposure?','Understated liabilities and a compliance exposure, because amounts owed to suppliers cannot be recognised as income merely by the passage of an internal deadline','None, since twelve months is prudent','Only a tax timing difference','A foreign exchange exposure','A','A credit balance is a liability to the supplier and an internal policy cannot extinguish it. Writing it to income overstates profit and creates a compliance exposure if the amounts are later claimed.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Internal Controls and Audit','exam','What is the purpose of a payment run pre-release review report?','To rank suppliers by size','To let an approver review the full proposed payment file for unusual payees, amounts and duplicates before funds move','To calculate accruals','To generate remittance advices','B','A pre-release review is the last preventive control before cash leaves. It exists so that anomalies are caught while the payment can still be stopped.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Invoice Processing','exam','A credit note from a supplier is received but the related invoice has already been paid. What is the correct action?','Ignore it, since the invoice is settled','Reduce a different supplier balance','Record the credit note and either offset it against the next payment or request a refund, tracking it until cleared','Post it to miscellaneous income','C','A credit note is a receivable from the supplier once the invoice is paid. It is recorded and tracked to clearance either by offset or refund, not written to income.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Vendor Master File Management','exam','Which field change in the vendor master should always trigger the highest level of verification?','The contact person name','The payment terms','The default currency','The bank account details','D','The bank account is the field that determines where money physically goes. It is the target of payment diversion fraud and therefore carries the strictest verification requirement.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Accounting and Month-End Close','exam','Payables must report the accrual for services received but not invoiced. Which source is the most reliable basis?','Purchase order commitments net of amounts already invoiced, confirmed with the budget owners','Last year figure adjusted for inflation','The average of the last three months payments','The supplier statement only','A','Open commitments net of invoicing represent the obligation actually incurred, and budget owners can confirm what was delivered. Historical averages do not evidence a present obligation.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Tax and Regulatory Compliance','exam','A UAE company imports a service from a non-resident supplier. What VAT mechanism generally applies?','No VAT, as the supplier is outside the UAE','The reverse charge, under which the recipient accounts for both output and input VAT on the return','Zero-rating with no entry required','Standard rating charged by the foreign supplier','B','The reverse charge shifts the accounting for VAT on imported services to the registered recipient. The output and input entries are made on the same return, with recovery depending on the use of the service.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Automation and AP Technology','exam','A supplier portal lets suppliers see invoice and payment status themselves. What is the main operational benefit to payables?','It removes the need for three-way matching','It eliminates approval workflows','It reduces inbound status enquiries and shifts data correction to the party that owns the data','It replaces the general ledger','C','Status enquiries are a large share of payables workload and are pure rework. A portal removes them and lets suppliers correct their own submission errors at source.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Internal Controls and Audit','hard','A benchmarking review shows cost per invoice of 14 against a peer median of 4, with 62% of invoices non-purchase-order. What is the most defensible improvement plan?','Cut payables headcount by two thirds to reach the median','Increase the approval threshold so fewer approvals are needed','Move payables to a lower-cost location','Attack the driver by extending purchase order coverage and supplier portal submission, then measure cost per invoice again once the mix has changed','D','The cost gap is driven by the exception-heavy non-purchase-order mix, not by staff productivity alone. Changing the mix is what moves the metric, and cutting headcount or approvals first would degrade control.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Invoice Processing','hard','An invoice matches the purchase order and the receipt but the unit price is 8% above the contracted price list. Automated matching passes it because price tolerance is set at 10%. What is the control weakness?','The tolerance is set above the level at which a contracted price deviation is material, so contract price variances pass unreviewed','Nothing; the tolerance was applied correctly','Three-way matching should be two-way','The receipt should be ignored','A','A tolerance wider than the commercial deviation it is meant to catch makes the control decorative. Tolerances must be set below the level at which a price variance matters, with contract price as the reference.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Accounting and Month-End Close','hard','The payables ledger sub-total does not agree to the control account by 3,400. What is the correct approach?','Post a balancing journal to the control account','Investigate to identify the specific transactions causing the difference, correct them at source, and only then confirm that the accounts agree','Change the ledger to report from the sub-ledger only','Write the difference to expenses if immaterial','B','A plug conceals whatever caused the break, which may be a posting error or a missing transaction. The difference is decomposed to the individual entries and corrected where it arose.'),
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Vendor Master File Management','exam','What is the purpose of periodically deactivating vendor records with no activity for a defined period?','To renegotiate terms','To free database space','To shrink the population of usable payee records, so a dormant record cannot be quietly reused for a fraudulent payment','To improve reporting speed','C','Every active vendor record is a possible payee. Deactivating dormant records reduces the attack surface and forces a fresh, controlled reactivation with verification.')
,
('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)','Payments and Fraud Prevention','exam','Why must the person who prepares the payment file be different from the person who releases it at the bank?','It speeds up the payment run','It reduces the number of bank accounts needed','It satisfies the bank fee schedule','Because preparation and release are the two steps that together move money, so splitting them means a fraudulent instruction requires collusion','D','Creating an instruction and executing it are the two acts that transfer funds. Assigning them to different people means no individual can complete a payment alone.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)');

-- ============================================================================
-- Proof: every bank in this batch must carry at least 40 questions on production.
-- ============================================================================
do $$
declare r record;
begin
  for r in
    select c.name, coalesce(q.n, 0) as n
    from (values
      ('CPA (Certified Public Accountant)'),
      ('CMA (Certified Management Accountant)'),
      ('ACCA (Association of Chartered Certified Accountants)'),
      ('APS (Accredited Payables Specialist)')
    ) as c(name)
    left join (
      select certification, count(*) as n
      from public.certification_exam_question
      group by certification
    ) q on q.certification = c.name
  loop
    if r.n < 40 then
      raise exception 'certification % has only % questions on production, 40 required', r.name, r.n;
    end if;
    raise notice 'certification % has % questions', r.name, r.n;
  end loop;
end $$;
